# How a CFF2 charstring may end, which needs no endchar: after an escape
# byte, inside a number, with return, or with endchar's accented form. Each
# draws `100 100 rmoveto 10 20 rlineto` first.
format cff2
glyph
end
glyph # 1: an escape byte at the end
  100 100 rmoveto 10 20 rlineto byte:12
end
glyph # 2: a three-byte number cut after its second byte
  100 100 rmoveto 10 20 rlineto byte:28 byte:1
end
glyph # 3: a 16.16 number cut after its third byte
  100 100 rmoveto 10 20 rlineto byte:255 byte:0 byte:1
end
glyph # 4: a two-byte number cut after its first byte
  100 100 rmoveto 10 20 rlineto byte:247
end
glyph # 5: return outside a subroutine
  100 100 rmoveto 10 20 rlineto return 30 30 rlineto
end
glyph # 6: endchar with four operands (CFF's accented form) after a line
  100 100 rmoveto 10 20 rlineto 50 20 65 194 endchar
end
glyph # 7: a number left on the stack at the end
  100 100 rmoveto 10 20 rlineto 5
end
